# Goal 11: Fix cacos/cacosf — NaN sign on real part

## Functions
`cacos` (complex double), `cacosf` (complex float)

## Source files
- C (double): /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/complexd/cacosd.c
- C (float): /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/complexf/cacosf.c
- Rust: /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s3/src/complexd.rs (cacos), /home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s3/src/complexf.rs (cacosf)

## Problem
cacos(NaN, NaN) real part:
- C: -nan (negative NaN, sign bit set)
- Rust: nan (positive NaN, sign bit clear)

The sign bit of NaN matters for bitwise comparison. The C implementation produces a negative NaN for the real part, likely via a specific computation path (e.g., pi - nan).

## What needs to change
Compare the NaN handling path in Rust cacos/cacosf against C. When both inputs are NaN, the C code likely computes `pi/2 - nan` or similar which preserves a negative NaN sign. The Rust implementation may handle NaN differently.

## Success Criteria
- `cacos (nan,nan)` = `-nan,nan` (bitwise exact, including NaN sign bits)
- `cacosf (nan,nan)` = `-nan,nan` (bitwise exact)
